Video One

Git is a version control software. It helps us track changes in our code. On Windows, we can use Git Bash. We can also use Git in the VS Code terminal.

pwd
It shows the current folder path.

git --version
It shows the installed Git version.

git status
It shows the current status of files.

git init
It creates a Git repository in the current folder.

git add file1.txt
It adds only file1.txt to the staging area.

git add .
It adds all changes from the current folder to the staging area.

git commit -m "first commit"
It saves the staged changes as a commit.

git log
It shows the commit history.

git push
It uploads local commits to GitHub.

Steps I followed:

1. Opened my project folder in VS Code.
2. Opened the terminal.
3. Used pwd to check the folder path.
4. Checked Git using git --version.
5. Used git status to check the files.
6. Used git init to create a repository.
7. Used git add to add files.
8. Checked the status again.
9. Used git commit to save the changes.
10. Used git push to upload the code to GitHub.
```
